// voice_tract.lib — ONE THROAT, and both singers are seated at it.
//
// A voice is the only instrument in the catalogue that everyone in the room has
// and can judge. Sampled, it is the flattest thing here: `solo_vox` and
// `ahh_choir` are six zones and one recording each, so a sung line is that one
// held "aah" transposed, at one dynamic, on one vowel, with the take's own
// vibrato baked in and beating against every other note. That is the squeak.
//
// pm.SFFormantModelBP is a SOURCE AND A TRACT: a glottal wave through five
// resonant bandpasses whose centre frequencies, gains and bandwidths are the
// CSOUND manual's measured formant tables for five voice types and five vowels,
// linearly interpolated — so the vowel is a continuous signal and a line can
// move THROUGH it, which is the thing a recording of a vowel can never do.
//
// THE LEAD AND THE CHOIR ARE THE SAME THROAT. They import this file; nothing
// below is duplicated in either, because two copies of a vocal tract is exactly
// how a lead and a choir stop being the same singer. (build.js writes every
// dsp/*.lib into libfaust's virtual filesystem before it compiles, which is
// what makes a local import resolve at all.)
//
// FOUR THINGS THIS ADDS TO THE LIBRARY MODEL, each because the bare call is
// not yet an instrument:
//
//   THE NORMALIZER. resonbp's Q runs to 30, so whether a harmonic happens to
//   land on a formant peak swings the tract 13.4 dB across two octaves —
//   measured, MIDI 48-79 on a tenor 'a'. That is an accident of pitch and not a
//   musical intention, and a singer whose every note is a different loudness is
//   unusable. voxAgc normalizes the tract's STEADY STATE (30 ms, before the
//   note envelope, on a source that never stops running, so there is nothing
//   for it to pump against) and the same sweep comes back inside 4.6 dB.
//
//   THE GLOTTAL TILT. The library's source is a raw sawtooth: a fixed −6 dB/oct
//   buzz at every dynamic. A real fold closes HARDER when it is pushed and
//   leaves more harmonic above the second formant, so `push` opens a two-pole
//   corner from 500 Hz to 6 kHz. Measured at equal loudness, spectral centroid
//   goes x1.27 (MIDI 55), x1.28 (62), x1.31 (69) between the softest dynamic
//   and the hardest, with the RMS moving 0.1 dB. The voice opens; it does not
//   just get louder — the same claim gtr_amp and mallet make, and the reason
//   velocity on a sampled vocal can only ever be a fader.
//
//   THE BREATH BEFORE THE VOWEL. `exType` is the library's voice/fricative
//   ratio, held at a constant in the ready-made _ui. Here it starts high and
//   falls over ~30 ms, which is an h before the note, and it is most of what
//   makes the ear hear a person rather than a filtered saw.
//
//   ...AND IT USED TO BE A BREATH AFTER THE VOWEL TOO, WHICH NOBODY ASKED FOR.
//   Paul, 2026-08-27, listening on staging: *"All the songs in Asia and so
//   forth have really heavy breathing in 1971. Like Iranian pop. Just two
//   heavy breaths dominating every measure."* Read literally, and it was
//   literal. The term was `0.55*(1 - smooth(gate))` and `si.smooth` is a
//   SYMMETRIC one-pole: it rises over 30 ms when the gate opens — the h this
//   paragraph claims — and it FALLS over 30 ms when the gate shuts, which
//   walks `fric` straight back up to `breath + 0.55` while the note is still
//   sounding through `voxEnv`'s release. voxAgc normalizes the tract's steady
//   state, so the aspiration that replaced the tone came out at the tone's own
//   loudness: a full-level exhale, as long as the genre's `rel`.
//     WHY THAT REGION AND NOT EVERYWHERE. Every anchor in the Asia/MENA block
//   seats `solo_vox` in chair one, they are BALLADS (`rel` 1.0-1.4 s: iranpop
//   1.1, kabulpop 1.2, nhacvang 1.3, arabesk 1.3, enka 1.4) and they are
//   legato (`maxHold` 3-4), so each bar holds one phrase with one clean start
//   and one clean end — an h in and a half-second exhale out. Two a measure,
//   which is the number Paul said. MEASURED on the rendered singer's stem,
//   8-16 kHz, swells standing 6 dB over the stem's own median in that band,
//   merged at 0.4 s: iranpop 1.92/bar, nhacvang 2.17, mandopop 2.17, dangdut
//   2.08, filmi 2.42, peaks +12 to +35 dB over the median. The fast records in
//   the same block (aljil rel 0.4, shaabi 0.5, khmerrock 0.5) hide it under
//   the next note, which is why it reads as a REGION and is really a release.
//     THE GATE'S DIAL IS NOT THE CAUSE AND WAS MEASURED FIRST. `breath` is the
//   genre's own `air` (genres.js MOUTHS -> to-engine voiceForInstr), and
//   sweeping it across its whole declared range on the rendered record moved
//   the stem's 8-16 kHz MEDIAN from -72.3 to -57.2 dB and left the swells
//   where they were: 2.12/bar at breath 0, 1.87/bar at 0.144, 1.75/bar at
//   0.6. `air` is the floor; this term is the breathing. Turning `air` down
//   would have made the record quieter and still breathing.
//     SO THE TERM IS ONE-SIDED NOW, `*gate`. While the gate is open it is
//   multiplication by 1.0 — bit-exact, so every sustained sample of every
//   voice in the catalogue is what it was — and while it is shut the
//   aspiration is gone and the release tail is the tone falling away.
//
//   A LITTLE FILTERING AND A LITTLE ROOM, ALWAYS, BECAUSE A MODELLED THROAT
//   HAS NEITHER. Paul, 2026-08-28: *"The modeled throat is okay but I think we
//   always need a tiny bit of filtering and reverb inside the voice itself to
//   smooth some of the natural weirdness of the synthesis."* Both halves of
//   that are `voxVoicing` and `voxRoom` below, and the scope is his word
//   INSIDE: they are the instrument's own voicing, not a send on the channel,
//   so they travel with the singer into every record and no hand dials them.
//   There is not one new param in this round.
//
//   THE VIBRATO THAT GROWS. A singer does not arrive vibrating; the wobble
//   comes in after the note is established and widens while it is held.
//   voxVib delays it by a breath and ramps it, and it modulates the SOURCE
//   ONLY — the formants are read at the note's nominal pitch, because a mouth
//   does not change shape at 5 Hz, and because feeding a modulated frequency
//   into the filterbank re-reads 15 interpolated formant tables every sample
//   and measured 2.2x the cost for a mouth that wobbles.
declare name "voice_tract";
import("stdfaust.lib");

// seconds since this gate opened; zero while it is shut
voxSince(gate) = (\(t). (gate * (t + 1.0/ma.SR))) ~ _;

// the vibrato a singer actually has: nothing for the first breath of the note,
// then in over `rise`
voxVib(depth, rise, t) = depth * min(1.0, max(0.0, (t - 0.12) / max(0.02, rise)));

// THE FOLD. `push` is how hard it is driven — the spectral tilt, not the level.
// The tilt is factored out because it is LINEAR: two cords sounding into one
// throat is the same signal as their sum through one fold, and the choir sums
// its pair before folding rather than carrying a second filter for it.
voxTilt(push) = fi.lowpass(2, 500.0 + push*push*5500.0);
voxGlottis(push, f) = os.sawtooth(f) : voxTilt(push);

// steady-state normalizer (see THE NORMALIZER above). Feed-forward, no envelope
// in its input, so it follows pitch and vowel and never the note.
voxAgc(target, tau, x) = x * gg
with {
  r  = x*x : si.smooth(ba.tau2pole(tau)) : sqrt;
  gg = target / max(0.0005, r) : min(80.0) : max(0.02) : si.smooth(ba.tau2pole(tau));
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE VOICING. A gentle fixed tilt, two corners, both constants, both here
// because the LIBRARY MODEL is wrong at those ends and not because a mix
// wanted them.
//
//   55 Hz, TWO POLES, BELOW A THROAT. `pm.formantFilterbankBP` is five
//   `fi.resonbp` in parallel and nothing else: there is no radiation term and
//   no highpass anywhere in it, so everything under the first formant is the
//   bandpass skirts, the sawtooth's own sub-fundamental leakage and voxAgc's
//   gain wander at 30 ms — none of which is a person. The corner is set by the
//   lowest note a modelled singer is ever SENT: state-engine's VOICE_TYPE puts
//   the bass compass at 82 Hz, and 55 is a fifth under that, so the deepest
//   fundamental in the catalogue loses 0.8 dB while 40 Hz — where Paul is
//   right that nothing sings — loses 6.6 and 20 Hz loses 17.6. Two poles
//   rather than one because the `fi.dcblocker` already at the end of every
//   voice module is a single pole at 35 Hz, and the point of this one is to be
//   steeper than that and to be INSIDE, where it also travels to whatever
//   seats the tract next.
//     AND IT IS INSURANCE RATHER THAN A CURE, WHICH IS WHAT THE MEASUREMENT
//   SAID. On the rendered singer this corner moves 20-80 Hz by 0.7 dB and on a
//   bass held at its floor note (82 Hz, the lowest VOICE_TYPE allows) by 0.6 —
//   because there was hardly anything there to take: a sixth-order bandpass on
//   the shipped module puts 20-80 Hz 46 dB under the whole signal for the
//   singer and 29 dB under it for the bass. (AND DO NOT ASK AN FFT. A Hann
//   window's skirts leak the first formant's 37 dB straight into a band 45 dB
//   below it, so a 4096-point Welch reads this band some 55 dB hot and its
//   deltas are the formant's, not the sub's. Measure the low end of a voice
//   with a steep IIR in the time domain; this round nearly filed a 1.3 dB rise
//   that the audio does not contain.)
//   The corner stays because eight chairs sum on a bus and because the next
//   instrument to sit at this tract may not be so well behaved, but nobody
//   should expect to HEAR it, and the smoothing Paul asked for is the other
//   two paragraphs.
//
//   4.2 kHz, ONE POLE, ABOVE THE TABLES. The buzz has two named sources and
//   both of them live above the highest formant the model carries. First,
//   `pm.SFFormantModel` mixes `exType` of RAW `no.noise` into the source —
//   white, flat to Nyquist, with no turbulence spectrum on it at all, which is
//   the whole of `fric` and therefore the whole of the breath. Second, the
//   filterbank is bandpasses, so the sawtooth partials above F5 are held up by
//   resonbp skirts rather than falling the way a real tract's higher poles
//   keep falling. Where "above the tables" is: the formant table tops out at
//   F5 4950 Hz for alto and soprano AT GAIN 0.001, and the highest formant
//   with real weight in it is the soprano's F4 at 3900 (gain 0.1) — so 4.2 kHz
//   is over every formant that carries anything, and one pole is the gentlest
//   slope that keeps falling instead of shelving out. It costs the soprano's
//   top formant 2.5 dB and takes 6.7 dB off 8 kHz and 11.9 off 16 kHz.
//
//   AND IT SITS BEFORE voxAgc ON PURPOSE. Source, tract, voicing, normalizer:
//   the thing whose steady state gets held at a fixed loudness should be the
//   FINISHED throat, so the fitted trims downstream (voice_lead's *3.7,
//   voice_choir's *1.4) still mean what they were measured to mean and this
//   round is a colour change and not a level change.
voxVoicing = fi.highpass(2, 55.0) : fi.lowpass(1, 4200.0);

// THE ROOM, and it is the SINGER'S and not the RECORD'S. The plate and the
// hall on the bus are the space the record was made in; a hand sets those per
// genre and they are already there. This is the other thing, the one a
// modelled throat cannot have: a mouth in air, with a body under it and
// something to stand in. Without it the model reads as a formant filter
// because it arrives with no early energy at all — measured on the shipped
// module, 40 ms after the gate shuts on a 20 ms release the output is 108 dB
// under the note, which is not a quiet room, it is a vacuum.
//
//   FOUR ALLPASSES AND NO COMB, which is what keeps it a room and not an
//   effect. A Schroeder diffuser is magnitude-flat: it spreads the note in
//   TIME without putting a resonance anywhere, so nothing here can add a note
//   the singer did not sing, and at 16% it cannot be heard as reverb — it is
//   heard as the buzz having an edge taken off it.
//   4.7 / 7.3 / 11.9 / 17.9 ms, mutually prime enough that the cascade has no
//   common period to ring on, and summing to about 42 ms of spread: Paul's
//   "a few tens of milliseconds", which is a person in a small space rather
//   than the plate two files over.
//   THE GAINS FALL (0.62 .. 0.50) so the density grows while the energy does
//   not: the tail is under -30 dB inside 100 ms and gone by 250.
//   THE WET IS DAMPED at 4.8 kHz, one pole, for the same reason as the
//   voicing: a room made of the model's own top end would hand back exactly
//   the buzz the voicing just took out.
//   AND THE WET IS HIGHPASSED AT 140 Hz, WHICH THE FIRST BUILD OF THIS FILE
//   DID NOT DO. An allpass cascade has unity gain at DC — H(0) = (1-a)/(1-a)
//   = 1, every section — so with no corner on it the wet arrives at 0 Hz
//   exactly in phase with the dry and the sum is 1.16 there, +1.29 dB: the
//   room handing the 55 Hz corner its work back, and putting sub under a
//   singer where the whole point was to take it out. It is also the physics —
//   4.7 to 17.9 ms of path is a space 1.6 to 6 m across, and a space has no
//   reflection to give at a wavelength longer than it is.
//   0.16 IS THE MIX, about -16 dB under the dry. Uncorrelated, that is +0.11
//   dB of level, so it does not move a record's balance.
//   AND IT IS CHEAP because it has to be — this is per voice, per chair, in
//   the ring, and the crackle cost a whole round to kill. Four delay lines
//   under 800 samples and five multiply-adds, against a filterbank that
//   interpolates fifteen tables a sample. MEASURED as its own module against a
//   null one, so the number is absolute and not a ratio of two noisy big ones:
//   the room is +2.07 ms per audio second and the voicing +0.88, the pair
//   +2.36 — 3.5% of voice_lead's own 68 ms, and 0.24% of one core per sounding
//   voice. The choir carries two of each (two throats) and pays 4.3%.
//   IT IS APPLIED AFTER THE AMPLITUDE ENVELOPE, in each voice module rather
//   than inside voxTract, and that is the one thing about it that is not
//   arbitrary. The tract's source never stops running — that is what lets
//   voxAgc normalize a steady state — so a room inside voxTract would be
//   multiplied by the gate on its way out and the note would end as abruptly
//   as it does now. After the envelope, the note ends and its room does not.
voxRoom = _ <: _, (diffuse : fi.highpass(2, 140.0) : fi.lowpass(1, 4800.0) : *(0.16)) :> _
with {
  ap(ms, g) = fi.allpass_comb(4096, int(ms*0.001*ma.SR), g);
  diffuse = ap(4.7, 0.62) : ap(7.3, 0.58) : ap(11.9, 0.54) : ap(17.9, 0.50);
};

// THE TRACT ITSELF. `fnom` is the note's nominal pitch — what the formant
// correction (autobend, vocal effort) is read at; `src` is the glottal wave,
// which may be detuned and wobbling away from it.
voxTract(voice, vowel, breath, fnom, src, gate) =
  pm.SFFormantModel(voice, vowel, fric, fnom, 0.7, src, pm.formantFilterbankBP, 0)
  : voxVoicing
  : voxAgc(0.10, 0.030)
with {
  // `*gate` is the whole of the 2026-08-27 fix (header, THE BREATH BEFORE THE
  // VOWEL): the h rides the gate OPENING and nothing rides it shutting. x*1.0
  // is exact in IEEE-754, so a sounding note is sample-for-sample what it was.
  // ...AND THE h RIDES A RAMP NOW, NOT A STEP (2026-09-02). Paul, having
  // listened to the deployed page: *"There's a lot of click when they 'talk'
  // though and overlap and we should smooth that."* `gate` is a one-sample
  // 0 -> 1, so this term put the aspiration's whole 0.55 on in ONE SAMPLE: a
  // broadband step in the noise gain at every note start. Under the old
  // envelope that was hidden (the amp was at zero there and climbing); under
  // `voxEnv` below, which no longer drops to zero when a note retriggers, it
  // would be a burst of hiss at the running note's own loudness. So the h now
  // opens over `voxOpen`'s 3 ms — audibly the same breath, with no edge in it.
  //   ONE-SIDED, EXACTLY AS THE 2026-08-27 FIX LEFT IT: `voxOpen` snaps to 0
  // the sample the gate shuts, so nothing rides the gate CLOSING and the
  // release tail is still the tone falling away.
  fric = min(1.0, breath + 0.55*voxOpen(0.003, gate)*(1.0 - (gate : si.smooth(ba.tau2pole(0.03)))));
};

// A GATE THAT OPENS OVER `sec` AND SHUTS IN ONE SAMPLE. The h above is the only
// caller: it wants the ASPIRATION ramped in and it must not be ramped out (see
// the 2026-08-27 fix), and a symmetric si.smooth cannot say that.
voxOpen(sec, gate) = (\(y). (ba.if(gate < 0.5, 0.0,
                      min(1.0, y + 1.0/max(1.0, sec*ma.SR))))) ~ _;

// the breath in and the breath out
//
// ---- IT USED TO CUT THE NOTE'S THROAT TO RETRIGGER IT (2026-09-02) ---------
// Paul, listening to the deployed composer: *"There's a lot of click when they
// 'talk' though and overlap and we should smooth that."* The click was here,
// and it was in every one of the three singers at once, because all three take
// their amplitude from this line.
//
// `en.asr` RESTARTS FROM ZERO. Its attack counter is `+(gate) ~ *(gate' >= gate)`
// — a rising edge zeroes it — and its release counter is zeroed while the gate
// is open, so on the sample a note retriggers the envelope is `0 - 0 = 0`
// whatever it was the sample before. MEASURED on the shipped module at the
// params softfolk's own singer sends (attack 0.03 s, release 1.0 s, three notes
// 8 ms apart, which is exactly what the scheduler writes for back-to-back
// notes — stream-renderer `offS = s + (dur - 0.008)`): the envelope was at
// 0.992 and the next sample it was 0.0008. Output RMS across the join went
// 0.0760 -> 0.0262 -> 0.0702, a 9.2 dB notch 30 ms wide punched into the middle
// of a sung phrase, once per note. That is the click; a mouth cannot do it.
//
// SO THE ENVELOPE IS A RAMP THAT REMEMBERS WHERE IT IS, and the ramp is
// raised-cosine at both ends. `u` walks toward the gate at 1/attack or
// 1/release per second and is CONTINUOUS across an edge — a retrigger during a
// release resumes from 0.992 instead of from 0 — and `0.5 - 0.5*cos(pi*u)` puts
// a smooth start and a smooth finish on every ramp, which is the declick.
// A gate held long enough saturates `u` at 1 and the shape at exactly 1.0, so a
// SUSTAINED note is the same number it always was; what changed is only the
// two edges and the join.
//
// THE 5 ms FLOOR is the other half of the declick: `attack` runs down to 0.002 s
// on the tract and 0.005 s on the singer, and 2 ms of ramp on a 130 Hz glottal
// pulse is a quarter of a period — audibly a step. Five is the shortest ramp
// that is a ramp at the bottom of a voice's compass and is still an onset
// rather than a swell.
//
// ABSENT-IS-TODAY IS SUSPENDED HERE, and Paul's sentence is the suspension:
// this changes the amplitude of every modelled vocal note in the catalogue and
// it is meant to. test/voice-smooth.test.js holds the before/after numbers.
voxEnv(attack, release, gate) = 0.5 - 0.5*cos(ma.PI*u)
with {
  up = 1.0 / max(1.0, max(0.005, attack)*ma.SR);
  dn = 1.0 / max(1.0, max(0.005, release)*ma.SR);
  u  = (\(y). (max(0.0, min(1.0, y + ba.if(gate > 0.5, up, 0.0 - dn))))) ~ _;
};

// ---- THE GRIT AND THE RESONANCE (2026-09-02) -------------------------------
// Paul, in the same sentence as the click: *"I wouldn't mind the voice having a
// tiny bit more grit and vocal resonance starting with his."* — "his" being
// softfolk's, the James Taylor row, which is the singer he named as the good
// one. This is that, and it is two stages and no new parameter, because it is
// the same kind of statement `voxVoicing` and `voxRoom` above already are: the
// instrument's own voicing, travelling with the singer into every record.
//
//   THE RESONANCE is a constant-Q peak at 2.6 kHz, +2.5 dB, Q 1.6. That is the
//   SINGER'S FORMANT band — the 2.4-3.2 kHz cluster a trained voice has and a
//   five-pole formant bank does not, because the CSOUND tables stop carrying
//   weight above the soprano's F4 at 3900 and `voxVoicing`'s own one-pole at
//   4.2 kHz is pulling the rest of it down. Q 1.6 is narrow enough to be a
//   resonance rather than a presence shelf, which is what "vocal resonance"
//   asks for and what a strip's +3 dB at 3 kHz is not.
//   THE GRIT is a tanh, level-compensated, so it is DISTORTION and not gain:
//   `tanh(k*x)/k` is 1.0 at small signals and softens the peaks.
//   `drv` IS AN ARGUMENT AND NOT A CONSTANT, and it has to be, because the two
//   callers hand this stage signals a factor of five apart. `voice_lead`
//   arrives through `voxAgc`, which holds the finished throat at 0.10 RMS
//   (~0.4 peak), so 1.9 puts the knee at tanh(0.76) — a few percent of third
//   harmonic and 1.5 dB off the peak. `tract_voice` has no normalizer at all
//   and its pre-trim signal is a fifth of that, so the same constant there was
//   not grit but a brick wall: MEASURED, tract_voice saturated outright (its
//   whole output pinned at 1/1.9 = 0.526) and the talking records lost 4.0-4.5
//   dB of their own 200 Hz-1 kHz tone. Each caller passes the number that puts
//   ITS signal on the same knee.
//
//   IT SITS AFTER voxTract AND BEFORE THE AMP, in the two LINE singers only:
//   voice_lead and tract_voice. NOT the choir — a room full of people is not
//   one throat being pushed, and a peak at 2.6 kHz on three detuned voices is a
//   ring, not a singer.
voxGrit(drv) = fi.peak_eq_cq(2.5, 2600.0, 1.6) : *(drv) : ma.tanh : /(drv);
